27 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085127 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ac67115-1932-54f2-8277-c9e7f1701f81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186085969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186085970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186085971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186085972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186085973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186085973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085968 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7f2db0c7-16fd-5c37-b274-a36499dcdd56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085969 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085969 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085969 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ef77e145-5232-5daf-8b5f-0fb282cf9be6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085970 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085970 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085970 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31d269e4-a2d4-5b5c-85aa-76e93abcc2d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085971 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085971 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085971 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3551a33d-6664-58b2-9a9b-881dde1b9a0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085972 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085972 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085972 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b0896f6-a2c8-565f-b6c2-08b572b5a62b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085973 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085973 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085973 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e44c0d4-a88e-511b-a1ea-ce56e6698eb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087437 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087437 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $-5x + y=3$\n\nII. $3x - y= -2$\n\nIII. $3x= 5$\n\nIV. $2x- 6y= 4$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5faa755c-1e2d-52a7-a521-49f79b94ec39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087437 #edb/ref 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-5$).\n\n- Equation II is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x- y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}-2\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3,$ $-1,$ and $-2$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3x}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{\\phantom{y}5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($3$).\n\n- Equation IV is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,-6,$ and $4)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087438 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087438 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $y = 7x+\\dfrac{2}{5}$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a4196bc-1f72-5435-9c00-c1fbef60b549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087438 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7x+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ y-7x & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+y & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $c$ is a fraction, not an integer. To fix this, we multiply by $5$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-y & =-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 5\\cdot (7x-y) & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ 35x-5y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $35x-5y=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087439 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14711" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087439 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $y-1=x$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087439 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e0c4000-c24e-5761-8cbe-60699b557eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087439 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by = c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\geq 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-x+y=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =1 \\\\ (-1)(-x+y) & =-1 \\\\ x-y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x-y=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087440 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087440 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the equation of the straight line that passes through the points $(-1,3)$ and $(2,4).$ Write the answer in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f78832f7-acd3-5779-b171-0650782b918e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087440 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-3}{2-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(2,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3\\cdot (y-4) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-12 & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-12 & =x-2 \\\\ 3y & =x+10 \\\\ -x+3y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+3y & =10 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+3y) & =(-1)\\cdot 10 \\\\ x-3y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-3y = -10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087709 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087710 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087711 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087712 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1045 "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087911 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cfe67386-85ee-52bc-a19f-9e5f91df386d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-208786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1071 "Express the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,4)$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b068d36-9b51-5e2a-ba98-4d4ce9e05360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088066 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{4-(-2)}{-1-(-3)}=\\frac{6}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-1,4).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =3(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y-4 & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =3x+7 \\\\ -3x+y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+y & =7 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-3x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 3x-y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is \n\n$$\n3x-y=-7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088219 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088219 #edb/ref 1069 "q-59668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088219 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088219 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $2x=3$\n\nII. $-x+2y=3$\n\nIII. $x-2y=0$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088219 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "079b3242-4c32-569f-b103-9bf2414bbafc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088219 #edb/ref 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2$ and $3$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($2$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{x-2y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{0}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $1,$ $-2,$ and $0$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1061 178720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f6a809a-5a39-577f-8881-246e8ed35867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088543 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1061 178721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1825121-20e1-5854-9a35-360f43ffb277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088544 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1061 178725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ad80f23-5479-5329-8fd1-39cf7d32c8f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088545 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1061 178726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "219a19a2-5ca7-55ec-b664-706e0003f956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088546 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1061 178730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54390c19-603c-54bd-bf54-163d31414029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1061 178733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5756f274-95fd-5077-9cb0-2b24aadadb72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1064 "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1065 9126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "739ff387-5940-547f-adac-8b2ac8e5a229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088991 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\ y-2x & =-1 \\\\ -2x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1065 9221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "914fb469-510e-5e74-92bc-6174870cbd47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088993 #edb/ref 1067 "Converting a Line to Standard Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493401 #edb/ref 1080 "y=3x-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493401 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493401 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c553f0-8dd8-5f5b-837c-09549d9227e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493412 #edb/ref 1080 "y-7x=-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cefc6ed-d53f-5610-b027-2f4d4f49fe33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493434 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493434 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493434 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1900311e-ec2f-5a19-8cfb-a76c9ae262ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493440 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493440 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493440 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1bbe4881-0890-557a-94a3-38fa413a495b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493453 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26f25391-da6a-50f7-9fbb-57467d22e097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493458 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493458 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493458 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "288dfc3c-caee-5e89-b10d-3410df396b1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493475 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493475 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493475 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "319cea52-16bc-509f-b234-b5a50371b20c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493481 #edb/ref 1080 "x+y=-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493481 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493481 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "378b3a35-e891-5069-bb1c-e487f7301f59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493482 #edb/ref 1080 "5x+2y=8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493482 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493482 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "37dc7839-3847-5f01-81ef-f713051f0840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493512 #ed