#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493864 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493864 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493864 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493864 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493864 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b236d4f2-5cba-5c8d-9d04-e7159dd85f16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186493787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186493787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493879 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e06d190d-4809-59ce-85bf-42f3c5088325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493891 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493891 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-5,5)$ and $(-1,6)$, expressed in standard form?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03ad1173-6dbd-512f-86a7-67df49248ff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493891 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-5}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(-5,5).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y-5) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(x+5) \\\\ 4y-20 & =x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-20 & =x+5 \\\\ 4y & =x+25 \\\\ -x+4y & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =25 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 25 \\\\ x-4y & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $x-4y=-25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493922 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $3x=y+1$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c641ffa-5f29-5085-9bd8-14bb6fe11280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493922 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-y=1.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-y=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493927 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $3y=5$\n\nII. $-x+7y=0$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=6$\n\nWhich of these lines is written in standard form?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61b55285-bdfc-5092-a032-79dc8a8f2ea3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493927 #edb/ref 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations.\n\n- Equation I is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{5}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $3$ and $5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($0$).\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-1$).\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{6}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $6$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493928 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $3y+3x+3=0$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62452c7f-a9e2-5c51-81ef-baa2650df43c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493928 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+3x+3 & =0 \\\\ 3x+3y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $3.$ So, we divide by $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{3x+3y}{3} & =\\frac{-3}{3} \\\\ x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+y=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1071 "Express the equation of the line $y=\\frac{2}{5}x-3$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d0eef6f-b433-56d1-a89d-4aab29bca392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493934 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{5}x+y=-3\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}x+y & =-3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}x-y & =3 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{2}{5}x-y) & =5\\cdot (3) \\\\ 2x-5y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x-5y=15.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109609" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493940 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $y=-7x-4$ in standard form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b7addf0-160c-54bc-8147-e2a24b57e5bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493940 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7x-4 \\\\ 7x+y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $7x+y=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186493843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493955 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equations of the following lines.\n\nI. $4x+2y=14$\n\nII. $5x=10$\n\nIII. $5x-3y=1$\n\nWhich of these equations are written in standard form?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c460c44e-72fe-595b-9fd7-cf8fb6438c3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493955 #edb/ref 1074 "The *standard form* equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a\\ge 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors greater than $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(4,2,$ and $14)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(5$ and $10)$ have a common factor of $5.$\n\n- Equation III is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{{5x-3y}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variables}}\\,={{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $5,$ $-3,$ and $1$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is positive ($5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
